okay we're going to be working with  finding a sinusoidal equation  given the maximum and the minimum and  how do we do that well you're already  familiar with graphing sine and cosine  so this is a little bit more challenging  because we're having to work backwards  to find the equation  i'm going to show you how to work with  these formulas as well as like a more  intuitive way of working with it  visually by graphing it so let's dive  into this um  video here the first thing is let's  graph  the max and the min so the max is at  negative 1 2  the minimum is at 1 negative 4 and so if  i was to draw  a sketch it would look something like  that now remember the sine and cosine  graph they keep going in both directions  forever and ever but what i want to  point out to you is when you go from the  maximum to the minimum  right maximum to the minimum notice that  this is  actually half of one period  so we can see from negative one to  positive one that's a distance of  two but the period would actually be  double it actually would be four  okay now the formula that we use to find  the b  value is this guy here period equals two  pi divided by b  so let's go ahead and do that to figure  out what b is so if the period is 4  4 is like 4 over 1 we can cross multiply  so  4b equals 2 pi  divide both sides by 4 and you can see  that b equals 2 pi over 4 which is like  pi  over 2 if we reduce okay so now we have  our b value  the next thing i like to do is i like to  find this midline  the midline is what kind of divides the  graph in half half the graph is above  half the graph is below normally that  midline falls right on the x-axis  but in this case let's see we're here at  negative 4  positive 2 that's the distance of 6. so  halfway would be  3 up or 3 down so that's going to put us  right here  and that's going to be our our new  midline but you can see that midline  instead of being at the x-axis  has actually shifted down 1 so that  means that our k value is equal to  negative 1.  now you can also use this formula max  plus min divided by two  so that'd be two plus negative four is  negative two divided by two is negative  one  so same answer now for the amplitude the  amplitude is like how high these waves  are  but that's from the perspective of the  midline like if you're on a beach you're  measuring how high the waves are it's  not like you're on a surfboard  you know and you're trying to measure  like from here down to here  so what you want to do is you can find  from that midline we're going up  one two three or going down one two  three so we know our amplitude  is equal to three but to use the formula  we take 2 minus negative 4 which is like  2  plus 4 which is 6 divided by 2 is 3 the  absolute value is always positive so you  get the same answer amplitude of 3.  now the next thing i like to do is do  the horizontal shift the phase shift  that's our h value in these equations  here  i like to start with the cosine graph  because cosine starts right at the  maximum here right on the y-axis  and it goes down to the minimum back up  to the maximum so here this  if we're doing a cosine graph you can  see normally the cosine would start here  it's shifting  left 1. so we're going to say that h is  equal to  negative 1 okay so let's put this all  together now  with the cosine graph we have y is equal  to  amplitude of 3 cosine  the b value is pi over 2 is shifting  left one so it's going to be x minus  negative one which is like x plus one  remember the one that's grouped with the  x here this has the opposite effect plus  one's going  left one if it was minus one it would go  right one and then we said that the  k value was down one so it shifted down  one  that's our new midline so this is our  cosine graph but you're probably saying  you know mario  how about the sine graph well the other  important thing that i want you to  realize is that from the max to the min  that we said that was half of a period  right but the difference between the  cosine graph and the sine graph is  they're out of phase by a quarter of a  period  see like how cosine starts right here if  you were to continue that graph to the  left  see now you can see this looks like a  sine graph right  so this is really like see if you were  to take this sine graph right here and  shift it left by a quarter of a period  it would end up see this point would be  right here and that would be a cosine  graph  so they're out of phase by a quarter of  a period in this case since the period  was  four a quarter of four is going to be  one so they're just out of  phase by one so what we would do now is  we would have to shift it to the  left one but when you do that left one  means you're actually going to be  adding one because this has the opposite  effect so now we can write our sine  graph  three sine pi over 2 x  plus 2 minus 1 and that's going to be  your sine graph  now again you can also add multiple  periods you could say if i add 4 here x  plus 6 or if you subtract 4  x minus 2 that would also be an equation  of the graph here and you've got it so  if you want to see another example i'll  put a link to the video that i did  another example with the same concept  right there  i'll see you over in that video
 

